<h2>The prosecution&rsquo;s case</h2>
<p>The case began on 29 August 2025, when 300 grams of heroin was allegedly seized from a co-accused, who named the petitioner as her supplier. A search of the petitioner&rsquo;s house followed, in which the police claimed recovery of 712 grams of heroin, and a third accused was arrested. The chargesheet invoked Sections 21, 25 and 29 of the NDPS Act. On the seizure memo&rsquo;s figures, this was comfortably a commercial-quantity case, attracting the twin conditions of Section 37 — the court must be satisfied that there are reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty and is not likely to offend on bail.</p>

<h2>What the FSL report actually said</h2>
<p>The defence pointed to the FSL report dated 7 April 2026: the bulk of the seized material did not test positive for diacetylmorphine at all, showing only degraded compounds. The State&rsquo;s answer was that the substance was degraded heroin and likely contained the drug. The court went with the laboratory, not the label:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment (para 12):</strong> on the forensic finding that only about 54 grams contained diacetylmorphine, &ldquo;the rigours of Section 37 of the NDPS Act are not applicable.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>The judgment drew on persuasive authority, including an Allahabad High Court decision of 2022 and an earlier Delhi High Court bail order, both cited in the order itself, treating the actual narcotic content established by analysis as what determines the quantity classification for bail purposes.</p>

<h2>The custody-and-trial arithmetic</h2>
<p>With Section 37 out of the way, the familiar bail considerations took over, and they ran in the petitioner&rsquo;s favour:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody.</strong> Eleven months and twenty-nine days in judicial custody, with earlier interim bail availed and the petitioner surrendering on its expiry — conduct the court expressly noted.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Stage of trial.</strong> In the court&rsquo;s words (para 14), &ldquo;The trial has not yet commenced and charges are yet to be framed&rdquo; — with twenty-three prosecution witnesses still to be examined.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Parity.</strong> A co-accused had been admitted to bail in March 2026, placing the petitioner&rsquo;s continued custody in an increasingly weak position.</div>
</div>
<p>The defence had also argued that no independent witnesses joined the house search and that videography of the search under Section 105 BNSS was not supplied; the State called the videography requirement directory. The court did not need to decide those points to grant bail — they remain live for trial.</p>

<h2>Why this matters in Delhi NDPS practice</h2>
<p>Seizure memos speak in gross weights; Section 37 operates on narcotic content. Where a quantitative FSL analysis shows the actual drug content below the commercial threshold, the entire complexion of a bail application changes — from the near-insurmountable twin conditions to the ordinary triad of flight risk, tampering and reoffending, each of which can be met by conditions. For defence counsel, obtaining and scrutinising the quantitative report is often the single most consequential step in an NDPS bail brief. For investigators, the decision underlines that the chemical analysis, not the panchnama, will carry the day in court.</p>
<p>The court imposed the usual architecture of conditions — attendance, address and mobile-number disclosure, no contact with witnesses, no fresh offences — on a bond of Rs 50,000 with one surety, and clarified that its observations were confined to the bail stage.</p>
